{#
/**
 * @file
 * Recipe teaser — the rows of the /recipes View (grid style, entity:node row).
 *
 * Renders the shared `recipe-card` Single Directory Component. The SDC owns the
 * markup, styles and props/slots contract; this template's only job is to map
 * node fields onto it. The exact same component renders on the full node page
 * (node--recipe.html.twig) — edit the component once, both update.
 *
 * The scalar props come from src/Hook/RecipeHooks.php rather than being read
 * out of `node.field_*` here, so the field names live in one place and this
 * template stays a mapping rather than a query.
 *
 * - The hero image goes into the `media` slot as a rendered field so its image
 *   style, alt text and cacheability are preserved; a placeholder fills the
 *   slot when field_hero is empty.
 * - Diet and ingredient terms go into the `tags` slot as `tag-pill` atoms
 *   (components inside a slot — the thing props can't do).
 *
 * @see template_preprocess_node()
 * @see \Drupal\flavourful\Hook\RecipeHooks
 */
#}
{#
  Every value here comes from RecipeHooks, never from `node.field_*` — see the
  comment on RecipeHooks::addRecipeProps() for why reading a field in Twig is
  not safe when it may be empty.

  The trailing `|filter` drops the nulls a sparse recipe produces (no cuisine
  term, no difficulty). SDC treats an absent optional prop and one passed as
  null differently: a prop declared `type: string` rejects the null outright.
  Filtering is what lets this map stay flat instead of becoming a chain of
  `{% if %}`-guarded merges, one per field a recipe might not have.
#}
{% set card_props = {
  title: label,
  url: url,
  eyebrow: recipe_eyebrow,
  summary: recipe_summary,
  meta: recipe_meta,
  heading_level: card_heading_level|default(3),
  variant: card_variant|default('compact'),
  difficulty: recipe_difficulty,
}|filter(v => v is not null) %}

{#
  `only` keeps this template's context out of the component, so anything the
  blocks below need is passed explicitly — see the same note in page.html.twig.
  Without it, a node variable sharing a prop name (`title`, `label`, `content`)
  would silently override what is set above.

  The node's own `attributes` *are* passed on purpose here: in teaser view mode
  the card is the node's entire rendered output, so node--type-recipe,
  recipe--quick and the contextual-links data belong on it. The full node page
  does the opposite and hands the card a fresh attributes object, because there
  the card is one element inside a larger article.
#}
{% embed 'flavourful:recipe-card' with card_props|merge({
  attributes: attributes,
  hero_media: content.field_hero,
  tag_items: recipe_tags,
}) only %}
  {% block media %}
    {% if hero_media|render|trim is not empty %}
      {{ hero_media }}
    {% else %}
      <span class="recipe-card__placeholder" aria-hidden="true">◍</span>
    {% endif %}
  {% endblock %}

  {#
    `tag_items`, not `tags` — the component's own slot is called `tags`, and a
    context variable of the same name would shadow it inside the component.
  #}
  {% block tags %}
    {% for tag in tag_items %}
      {{ include('flavourful:tag-pill', {
        label: tag.label,
        url: tag.url,
      }, with_context = false) }}
    {% endfor %}
  {% endblock %}
{% endembed %}
